<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="eigen-spaces-complete">
    <title>Eigenspaces (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We have some understanding of what eigenvectors and eigenvalues are, and why they matter. Now we'll learn
                        how to find them in the finite-dimensional case using what we know about solving linear systems of equations.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Finding Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                Given an <m>n \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, find non-zero <m>\xvec</m> and scalar <m>\lambda</m> such that
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>A\xvec = \lambda \xvec</m>. This simplifies as
                        <md>
                            <mrow>A\xvec - \lambda \xvec  \amp= \mathbf{0} </mrow>
                            <mrow>(A - \lambda I_n)\xvec \amp=\mathbf{0} </mrow>
                        </md>
                        If <m>\text{det}(A-\lambda I_n) \neq 0</m>, then <m>A-\lambda I_n</m> is invertible, and there is 
                        a unique solution (<m>\xvec = \mathbf{0}</m>). However, if <m>\text{det}(A-\lambda I_n) = 0</m>,
                        then <m>A-\lambda I_n</m> is not invertible, and there are infinitely many solutions. 
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        So given <m>A</m>, the problem reduces to finding <m>\lambda</m> such that <m>\text{det}(A-\lambda I_n) = 0</m>.
                        This computation gives a polynomial of degree <m>n</m>, called the <term>characteristic polynomial of</term> <m>A</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Example: Finding Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>
           <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
           </statement> 
           <solution>
            <p>
                The matrix <m>A-\lambda I_2</m> is <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 - \lambda \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 1 - \lambda \end{bmatrix}</m>, which
                has determinant <m>(1-\lambda)^2 - 4 = \lambda^2 - 2\lambda -3 = (\lambda-3)(\lambda + 1)</m>. So the eigenvalues of 
                <m>A</m> are <m>3, -1</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                To find the associated eigenvectors, we take each eigenvalue one at a time. If <m>\lambda =3</m>, then the matrix
                <m>A-3I_2</m> is <m>\begin{bmatrix} -2 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and we need to find all <m>\xvec</m>
                such that
                <md>
                    \begin{bmatrix} -2 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix} \xvec  = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>.
                This is a matrix equation representing a linear system, so we can use RREF of the associated augmented matrix 
                to find the solution space:
                <md>
                     \left[ \begin{array}{rr|r} -2 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp -2 \amp 0 \end{array} \right] \to \left[ \begin{array}{rr|r} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0  \end{array} \right]
                    
                </md>
                This corresponds to the solution space
                <md>
                    E_3 = \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \R \right\}
                </md>,
                called the <term>eigenspace</term> associated with <m>\lambda =3</m>.
                
                
            </p>
            <p>
                If <m>\lambda = -1</m>, we can follow work similar to the above to obtain
                <md>
                    E_{-1} =  \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \R \right\}
                </md>
                
            </p>
           </solution>
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title> More Eigenvalue Examples</title>
        <task workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Let  <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Reasoning geometrically, we can see the eigenvalues are <m>\lambda = 1, -1</m>, with 
                    eigenspaces 
                    <md>
                    E_{1} =  \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \R \right\}
                    </md>
                    and 
                    <md>
                    E_{-1} =  \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \R \right\}
                    </md>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Let  <m>B = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -i \\ i \amp 0\end{bmatrix}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The characteristic polynomial is <m>\lambda^2 - 1</m>, so the eigenvalues are <m>1,-1</m>. The augmented
                    matrices needed to find the eigenvectors are complex-valued, but we can still use technology to find RREF 
                    and conclude that 
                     <md>
                    E_{1} =  \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} -i \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \C \right\}
                    </md>
                    and 
                    <md>
                    E_{-1} =  \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} i \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \C \right\}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Let  <m>C = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The eigenvalues are <m>\pm i</m>, and 
                     <md>
                    E_{i} =  \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} i \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \C \right\}
                    </md>
                    and 
                    <md>
                    E_{-i} =  \left\{ c \begin{bmatrix} -i \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} : c \in \C \right\}
                    </md>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
</page>

<page>
    <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Summary</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Diagonal matrices show their eigenvalues on the diagonal (matrix <m>A</m>).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Some transformations on vector spaces over <m>\R</m> have no real eigenvalues (matrix <m>C</m>).
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Some transformations on vector spaces over <m>\C</m> have <em>only</em> real eigenvalues, 
                            and in quantum mechanics, this is good! More specifically, if <m>A: V \to V</m> 
                            (<m>V</m> a vector space over <m>\C</m>)is Hermitian, then the eigenvalues of <m>A</m>
                            are all real!
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            This matters because a linear operator <m>A</m> represents observables, and eigenvalues of these
                            operators are the possible observed measurements; physical measurements should be real-valued!
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            This is why observables in quantum mechanics are represented by linear, Hermitian operators.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
        
    </exercise>
</page>


       
    </worksheet>
